{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef sum_product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of integers, return a tuple consisting of a sum and a product of all the integers in a list.\n    Empty sum should be equal to 0 and empty product should be equal to 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return (0, 1)\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    total_product = 1\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        total_sum += num\n        total_product *= num\n\n    return (total_sum, total_product)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n    >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n    [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    rolling_max_list: List[int] = []\n    current_max: int = float('-inf')\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        if number > current_max:\n            current_max = number\n        rolling_max_list.append(current_max)\n\n    return rolling_max_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "```python\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return \"\"\n\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        # Check if the suffix starting from index i is a palindrome\n        if is_palindrome(string[i:]):\n            # If it is, this is the longest palindromic suffix.\n            # Take the prefix before this suffix, reverse it, and append to the original string.\n            prefix_to_reverse = string[:i]\n            return string + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n\nclass MalformedParenthesesError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for malformed parentheses groups.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\n\nclass ParenthesesParser:\n    \"\"\"Parses a single parentheses group string and calculates its maximum nesting level.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, group_string: str):\n        self._group_string = group_string\n\n    def parse(self) -> int:\n        current_level = 0\n        max_level = 0\n\n        if not self._group_string.strip():\n            return 0\n\n        for char in self._group_string:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_level += 1\n                max_level = max(max_level, current_level)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_level -= 1\n                if current_level < 0:\n                    raise MalformedParenthesesError(f\"Unmatched closing parenthesis in group: '{self._group_string}'\")\n            elif not char.isspace():\n                raise MalformedParenthesesError(f\"Invalid character '{char}' in group: '{self._group_string}'\")\n\n        if current_level != 0:\n            raise MalformedParenthesesError(f\"Unmatched opening parenthesis in group: '{self._group_string}'\")\n\n        return max_level\n\n\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses separated by spaces.\n    For each of the group, output the deepest level of nesting of parentheses.\n    E.g. (()()) has maximum two levels of nesting while ((())) has three.\n    \"\"\"\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n    results = []\n    for group_str in groups:\n        parser = ParenthesesParser(group_str)\n        results.append(parser.parse())\n    return results"}
