{"task_id": "HumanEval/59", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_prime_factor(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the largest prime factor of n. Assume n > 1 and is not a prime.\n    This function iteratively divides n by its smallest prime factors until n becomes prime or 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_factor = 1\n\n    # Divide by 2 until n is odd\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        largest_factor = 2\n        n //= 2\n\n    # n must be odd at this point, so we can skip even numbers\n    # Iterate from 3 up to sqrt(n)\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= n:\n        while n % i == 0:\n            largest_factor = i\n            n //= i\n        i += 2  # Only check odd numbers\n\n    # If n is still greater than 2, then it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 2:\n        largest_factor = n\n\n    return largest_factor\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/61", "solution": "```python\ndef correct_bracketing(brackets: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string of '(' and ')' has correctly balanced and nested brackets.\n    Returns True if every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    bracket_count = 0\n    for char in brackets:\n        if char == '(':\n            bracket_count += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            bracket_count -= 1\n\n        # If the count drops below zero, a closing bracket appeared without a matching opening one.\n        if bracket_count < 0:\n            return False\n\n    # For correct bracketing, the final count must be zero (all brackets matched).\n    return bracket_count == 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/66", "solution": "```python\ndef digitSum(s):\n    \"\"\"Task\n    Write a function that takes a string as input and returns the sum of the upper characters only'\n    ASCII codes.\n\n    Examples:\n        digitSum(\"\") => 0\n        digitSum(\"abAB\") => 131\n        digitSum(\"abcCd\") => 67\n        digitSum(\"helloE\") => 69\n        digitSum(\"woArBld\") => 131\n        digitSum(\"aAaaaXa\") => 153\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for char in s:\n        if char.isupper():\n            total_sum += ord(char)\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/62", "solution": "```python\ndef derivative(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs represent coefficients of a polynomial: xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[2] * x^2 + ....\\n    Returns the derivative of this polynomial in the same form, or an empty list for constant/empty polynomials.\\n    >>> derivative([3, 1, 2, 4, 5])\n    [1, 4, 12, 20]\n    >>> derivative([1, 2, 3])\n    [2, 6]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not xs or len(xs) == 1:\n        return []\n\n    return [i * coeff for i, coeff in enumerate(xs[1:], start=1)]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/63", "solution": "def fibfib(n: int, _memo: dict = {0: 0, 1: 0, 2: 1}):\n    \"\"\"The FibFib number sequence is a sequence similar to the Fibbonacci sequnece that's defined as follows:\n    fibfib(0) == 0\n    fibfib(1) == 0\n    fibfib(2) == 1\n    fibfib(n) == fibfib(n-1) + fibfib(n-2) + fibfib(n-3).\n    Please write a function to efficiently compute the n-th element of the fibfib number sequence.\n    >>> fibfib(1)\n    0\n    >>> fibfib(5)\n    4\n    >>> fibfib(8)\n    24\n    \"\"\"\n    if n in _memo:\n        return _memo[n]\n\n    result = fibfib(n - 1, _memo) + fibfib(n - 2, _memo) + fibfib(n - 3, _memo)\n    _memo[n] = result\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/65", "solution": "```python\ndef circular_shift(x, shift):\n    \"\"\"Circular shift the digits of the integer x, shift the digits right by shift\n    and return the result as a string.\n    If shift > number of digits, return digits reversed.\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 1)\n    \"21\"\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 2)\n    \"12\"\n    \"\"\"\n    s = str(x)\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if shift > n:\n        return s[::-1]\n\n    effective_shift = shift % n\n    if effective_shift == 0:\n        return s\n\n    return s[-effective_shift:] + s[:-effective_shift]\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/64", "solution": "```python\ndef vowels_count(s):\n    \"\"\"Write a function vowels_count which takes a string representing\n    a word as input and returns the number of vowels in the string.\n    Vowels in this case are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'. Here, 'y' is also a\n    vowel, but only when it is at the end of the given word.\n\n    Example:\n    >>> vowels_count(\"abcde\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"ACEDY\")\n    3\n    >>> vowels_count(\"\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"rhythm\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"aeiou\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"AEIOU\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"mystery\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"python\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"a\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"b\")\n    0\n    >>> vowels_count(\"y\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"ProgrammingIsFun\")\n    5\n    >>> vowels_count(\"syzygy\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"happy\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"hYppy\")\n    1\n    >>> vowels_count(\"rhythms\")\n    0\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    VOWELS_SET = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n    s_lower = s.lower()\n\n    for i, char in enumerate(s_lower):\n        if char in VOWELS_SET:\n            count += 1\n        elif char == 'y' and i == len(s_lower) - 1:\n            count += 1\n\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "def search(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    You are given a non-empty list of positive integers. Return the greatest integer that is greater than\n    zero, and has a frequency greater than or equal to the value of the integer itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    frequencies = {}\n    for num in lst:\n        frequencies[num] = frequencies.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n    qualifying_integers = []\n    for num, freq in frequencies.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            qualifying_integers.append(num)\n\n    if not qualifying_integers:\n        return -1\n    else:\n        return max(qualifying_integers)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Strange sorting, is when you start with the minimum value,\n    then maximum of the remaining integers, then minimum and so on.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    sorted_temp = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while sorted_temp:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            result.append(sorted_temp.pop(0))\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_temp.pop())\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "def pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    \"Given an array representing a branch of a tree that has non-negative integer nodes\n    your task is to pluck one of the nodes and return it.\n    The plucked node should be the node with the smallest even value.\n    If multiple nodes with the same smallest even value are found return the node that has smallest index.\n\n    The plucked node should be returned in a list, [ smalest_value, its index ],\n    If there are no even values or the given array is empty, return [].\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = float('inf')\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for i, node_value in enumerate(arr):\n        # Check if the node_value is even\n        if node_value % 2 == 0:\n            if node_value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = node_value\n                smallest_even_index = i\n            elif node_value == smallest_even_value:\n                # If values are equal, choose the one with the smallest index\n                if i < smallest_even_index:\n                    smallest_even_index = i\n\n    if smallest_even_index == -1:\n        return []\n    else:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fruit_distribution(s,n):\n    \"\"\"\n    In this task, you will be given a string that represents a number of apples and oranges\n    that are distributed in a basket of fruit this basket contains\n    apples, oranges, and mango fruits. Given the string that represents the total number of\n    the oranges and apples and an integer that represent the total number of the fruits\n    in the basket return the number of the mango fruits in the basket.\n    for examble:\n    fruit_distribution(\"5 apples and 6 oranges\", 19) ->19 - 5 - 6 = 8\n    fruit_distribution(\"0 apples and 1 oranges\",3) -> 3 - 0 - 1 = 2\n    fruit_distribution(\"2 apples and 3 oranges\", 100) -> 100 - 2 - 3 = 95\n    fruit_distribution(\"100 apples and 1 oranges\",120) -> 120 - 100 - 1 = 19\n    \"\"\"\n    FRUIT_PATTERN = r'(\\d+)\\s+apples\\s+and\\s+(\\d+)\\s+oranges'\n    match = re.search(FRUIT_PATTERN, s)\n\n    # According to the problem statement's constraints \"Do NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions for edge cases\",\n    # we assume the input string 's' will always conform to the expected pattern.\n    # Therefore, 'match' is guaranteed to be a valid match object.\n\n    apples = int(match.group(1))\n    oranges = int(match.group(2))\n\n    return n - (apples + oranges)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate triangle: all sides must be positive and satisfy triangle inequality\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0):\n        return -1\n    if not ((a + b > c) and (a + c > b) and (b + c > a)):\n        return -1\n\n    # Calculate semi-perimeter\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate area using Heron's formula\n    area = math.sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))\n\n    # Round the area to 2 decimal places\n    return round(area, 2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/73", "solution": "```python\ndef smallest_change(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of elements that need to be changed to make the array palindromic.\n    It uses a two-pointer approach from both ends of the array.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if arr[left] != arr[right]:\n            changes += 1\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n    return changes\n```"}
