{"task_id": "HumanEval/77", "solution": "```python\ndef iscube(a):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an integer 'a' is a cube of some integer number.\n    Handles positive, negative, and zero inputs using floating-point cube root and rounding.\n    \"\"\"\n    if a == 0:\n        return True\n\n    sign = 1 if a >= 0 else -1\n    abs_a = abs(a)\n\n    # Calculate the cube root of the absolute value and round to the nearest integer.\n    root = round(abs_a**(1/3))\n\n    # Cube the rounded root and apply the original sign.\n    cubed_root = root**3 * sign\n\n    return cubed_root == a\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\nimport itertools\n\ndef is_prime(n):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime.\"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef generate_primes_up_to(limit):\n    \"\"\"Generates a list of prime numbers up to the given limit using a sieve.\"\"\"\n    primes = []\n    if limit < 2:\n        return primes\n\n    is_prime_sieve = [True] * (limit + 1)\n    is_prime_sieve[0] = is_prime_sieve[1] = False\n\n    for p in range(2, int(limit**0.5) + 1):\n        if is_prime_sieve[p]:\n            for multiple in range(p * p, limit + 1, p):\n                is_prime_sieve[multiple] = False\n\n    for p in range(2, limit + 1):\n        if is_prime_sieve[p]:\n            primes.append(p)\n    return primes\n\ndef is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that returns true if the given number is the multiplication of 3 prime numbers\n    and false otherwise.\n    Knowing that (a) is less then 100.\n    Example:\n    is_multiply_prime(30) == True\n    30 = 2 * 3 * 5\n    \"\"\"\n    # The smallest product of 3 distinct prime numbers is 2 * 3 * 5 = 30.\n    if a < 30:\n        return False\n\n    # Given 'a' is less than 100, the largest possible prime factor p3\n    # in a product p1 * p2 * p3 = a (with p1=2, p2=3) must satisfy p3 <= a / 6.\n    # For example, if a = 99, p3 <= 99 / 6 = 16.5. So primes up to 16 are sufficient.\n    primes = generate_primes_up_to(a // 6 + 1)\n\n    for p1, p2, p3 in itertools.combinations(primes, 3):\n        if p1 * p2 * p3 == a:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/76", "solution": "```python\ndef is_simple_power(x, n):\n    \"\"\"Your task is to write a function that returns true if a number x is a simple\n    power of n and false in other cases.\n    x is a simple power of n if n**int=x\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 1:\n        return True\n    if n == 1 and x != 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 0 or x <= 0:\n        return False\n\n    current_power = n\n    while current_power < x:\n        current_power *= n\n\n    return current_power == x\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/78", "solution": "def hex_key(num):\n    \"\"\"Counts the number of prime hexadecimal digits in a given hexadecimal string.\n    Prime hexadecimal digits are 2, 3, 5, 7, B (decimal 11), D (decimal 13).\n    \"\"\"\n    prime_hex_decimal_values = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13}\n    prime_count = 0\n    for digit in num:\n        decimal_value = int(digit, 16)\n        if decimal_value in prime_hex_decimal_values:\n            prime_count += 1\n    return prime_count"}
