{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "```python\ndef specialFilter(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts elements in the array that are greater than 10 and have both\n    first and last digits odd.\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n\n    def is_odd(digit):\n        return digit % 2 != 0\n\n    def get_first_digit(num):\n        # Extracts the first digit of a number, ignoring its sign.\n        # Uses string conversion for simplicity.\n        return int(str(abs(num))[0])\n\n    def get_last_digit(num):\n        # Extracts the last digit of a number, ignoring its sign.\n        return abs(num) % 10\n\n    for num in nums:\n        if num > 10:\n            first_digit = get_first_digit(num)\n            last_digit = get_last_digit(num)\n            if is_odd(first_digit) and is_odd(last_digit):\n                count += 1\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "```python\ndef bf(planet1, planet2):\n    '''\n    There are eight planets in our solar system: the closerst to the Sun\n    is Mercury, the next one is Venus, then Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn,\n    Uranus, Neptune.\n    Write a function that takes two planet names as strings planet1 and planet2.\n    The function should return a tuple containing all planets whose orbits are\n    located between the orbit of planet1 and the orbit of planet2, sorted by\n    the proximity to the sun.\n    The function should return an empty tuple if planet1 or planet2\n    are not correct planet names.\n    Examples\n    bf(\"Jupiter\", \"Neptune\") ==> (\"Saturn\", \"Uranus\")\n    bf(\"Earth\", \"Mercury\") ==> (\"Venus\")\n    bf(\"Mercury\", \"Uranus\") ==> (\"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\")\n    '''\n    PLANETS_ORDER = [\n        \"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\",\n        \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\"\n    ]\n\n    try:\n        idx1 = PLANETS_ORDER.index(planet1)\n        idx2 = PLANETS_ORDER.index(planet2)\n    except ValueError:\n        return ()\n\n    start_idx = min(idx1, idx2)\n    end_idx = max(idx1, idx2)\n\n    intermediate_planets = PLANETS_ORDER[start_idx + 1:end_idx]\n\n    return tuple(intermediate_planets)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef get_max_triples(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) from array a where i < j < k,\n    and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is a multiple of 3, with a[x] = x*x - x + 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # Helper function for combinations (n choose k)\n    # The math.comb function is used as it's standard library and efficient.\n    def combinations(n_val, k_val):\n        if k_val < 0 or k_val > n_val:\n            return 0\n        return math.comb(n_val, k_val)\n\n    count_rem_0 = 0\n    count_rem_1 = 0\n\n    for x in range(1, n + 1):\n        # We observe that a[x] = x*x - x + 1 follows a pattern modulo 3:\n        # If x % 3 == 2, then a[x] % 3 == 0.\n        # Otherwise (if x % 3 == 0 or x % 3 == 1), a[x] % 3 == 1.\n        if x % 3 == 2:\n            count_rem_0 += 1\n        else:\n            count_rem_1 += 1\n\n    # A sum of three numbers (a[i] + a[j] + a[k]) is a multiple of 3 if\n    # all three numbers have remainder 0 (0+0+0 = 0 mod 3), or\n    # all three numbers have remainder 1 (1+1+1 = 3 = 0 mod 3).\n    # Other combinations (like 0+0+1 or 0+1+1) do not sum to 0 mod 3.\n\n    triples_from_rem_0 = combinations(count_rem_0, 3)\n    triples_from_rem_1 = combinations(count_rem_1, 3)\n\n    return triples_from_rem_0 + triples_from_rem_1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "```python\ndef test_order_by_points():\n    # Test case from the problem description: empty list\n    assert order_by_points([]) == []\n\n    # Test case from the problem description,\n    # with tie-breaking resolved by ascending original index as per design document's interpretation.\n    # Original: [1 (sum 1, idx 0), 11 (sum 2, idx 1), -1 (sum 1, idx 2), -11 (sum 2, idx 3), -12 (sum 3, idx 4)]\n    # Sorted by (sum_of_digits, original_index, number):\n    # (1, 0, 1)   -> 1\n    # (1, 2, -1)  -> -1\n    # (2, 1, 11)  -> 11\n    # (2, 3, -11) -> -11\n    # (3, 4, -12) -> -12\n    # Expected output based on rules: [1, -1, 11, -11, -12]\n    assert order_by_points([1, 11, -1, -11, -12]) == [1, -1, 11, -11, -12]\n\n    # Additional test cases from the design document.\n\n    # Simple case with mixed positive, sums, and indices\n    # Input: [10, 2, 3, 1]\n    # Sums (and original indices):\n    # 10 (sum 1, idx 0)\n    # 2 (sum 2, idx 1)\n    # 3 (sum 3, idx 2)\n    # 1 (sum 1, idx 3)\n    # Sorted: [(1, 0, 10), (1, 3, 1), (2, 1, 2), (3, 2, 3)]\n    # Expected output: [10, 1, 2, 3]\n    assert order_by_points([10, 2, 3, 1]) == [10, 1, 2, 3]\n\n    # All elements have the same sum of digits, so should be sorted by original index\n    # Input: [1, 10, 100] (all sum to 1)\n    # Original indices: 1(0), 10(1), 100(2)\n    # Expected: [1, 10, 100] (maintains original order)\n    assert order_by_points([1, 10, 100]) == [1, 10, 100]\n\n    # Test with negative numbers only\n    # Input: [-1, -10, -100] (all sum to 1)\n    # Original indices: -1(0), -10(1), -100(2)\n    # Expected: [-1, -10, -100] (maintains original order)\n    assert order_by_points([-1, -10, -100]) == [-1, -10, -100]\n\n    # Test with zero\n    assert order_by_points([5, 0, 1]) == [0, 1, 5]\n\n    # Test with single element\n    assert order_by_points([42]) == [42]\n\n    # Test with various sums and no ties\n    assert order_by_points([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3]\n\n    # Test with negative numbers and different sums\n    # Input: [-12, -3, -1]\n    # Sums (and original indices):\n    # -12 (sum 3, idx 0)\n    # -3 (sum 3, idx 1)\n    # -1 (sum 1, idx 2)\n    # Sorted: [(1, 2, -1), (3, 0, -12), (3, 1, -3)]\n    # Expected: [-1, -12, -3]\n    assert order_by_points([-12, -3, -1]) == [-1, -12, -3]\n\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/150", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef x_or_y(n, x, y):\n    \"\"\"Returns x if n is a prime number, otherwise returns y.\"\"\"\n\n    is_n_prime = True\n    if n <= 1:\n        is_n_prime = False\n    elif n == 2:\n        is_n_prime = True\n    elif n % 2 == 0: # All other even numbers are not prime\n        is_n_prime = False\n    else:\n        # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of n\n        # Iterate by 2 to only check odd numbers\n        limit = int(math.sqrt(n))\n        for i in range(3, limit + 1, 2):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                is_n_prime = False\n                break\n\n    if is_n_prime:\n        return x\n    else:\n        return y\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/151", "solution": "```python\ndef double_the_difference(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return the sum of squares of the numbers\n    in the list that are odd. Ignore numbers that are negative or not integers.\n    '''\n    total_sum_of_squares = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        # Check if the number is an integer, positive, and odd\n        if isinstance(num, int) and num > 0 and num % 2 != 0:\n            total_sum_of_squares += num * num\n    return total_sum_of_squares\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/152", "solution": "```python\ndef compare(game,guess):\n    \"\"\"I think we all remember that feeling when the result of some long-awaited\n    event is finally known. The feelings and thoughts you have at that moment are\n    definitely worth noting down and comparing.\n    Your task is to determine if a person correctly guessed the results of a number of matches.\n    You are given two arrays of scores and guesses of equal length, where each index shows a match.\n    Return an array of the same length denoting how far off each guess was. If they have guessed correctly,\n    the value is 0, and if not, the value is the absolute difference between the guess and the score.\n\n\n    example:\n\n    compare([1,2,3,4,5,1],[1,2,3,4,2,-2]) -> [0,0,0,0,3,3]\n    compare([0,5,0,0,0,4],[4,1,1,0,0,-2]) -> [4,4,1,0,0,6]\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(game) != len(guess):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists 'game' and 'guess' must be of equal length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    # Iterate through both lists simultaneously to compare scores and guesses.\n    for i in range(len(game)):\n        score = game[i]\n        current_guess = guess[i]\n        if score == current_guess:\n            result.append(0)\n        else:\n            result.append(abs(score - current_guess))\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "def even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer. return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\"\"\"\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    # Process the absolute value of the number to handle negative inputs\n    s_num = str(abs(num))\n\n    for digit_char in s_num:\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 == 0:\n            even_count += 1\n        else:\n            odd_count += 1\n\n    return (even_count, odd_count)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/154", "solution": "```python\ndef cycpattern_check(a , b):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the second word or any of its rotations is a substring in the first word.\n    Handles edge cases for empty strings and length comparisons.\"\"\"\n    if not b:\n        return True # An empty string is a substring of any string.\n\n    # If b is longer than a, it cannot be a substring.\n    if len(b) > len(a):\n        return False\n\n    # Generate all unique cyclic rotations of b.\n    rotations = set()\n    for i in range(len(b)):\n        rotated_b = b[i:] + b[:i]\n        rotations.add(rotated_b)\n\n    # Check if any rotation is a substring of a.\n    for rotation in rotations:\n        if rotation in a:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/153", "solution": "def Strongest_Extension(class_name, extensions):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the 'strength' of each extension and finds the strongest one.\n    Strength is defined as (uppercase letters - lowercase letters).\n    Ties are broken by choosing the first appearing extension.\n    Returns a string in the format 'ClassName.StrongestExtensionName'.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class Extension:\n        def __init__(self, name, strength):\n            self._name = name\n            self._strength = strength\n\n        def get_name(self):\n            return self._name\n\n        def get_strength(self):\n            return self._strength\n\n    class ExtensionCalculator:\n        def calculate_strength(self, extension_name):\n            uppercase_count = sum(1 for char in extension_name if char.isupper())\n            lowercase_count = sum(1 for char in extension_name if char.islower())\n            return uppercase_count - lowercase_count\n\n    class StrongestExtensionFinder:\n        def __init__(self, calculator):\n            self._calculator = calculator\n\n        def find(self, class_name_local, extensions_local):\n            strongest_ext_obj = self._find_strongest_extension_object(extensions_local)\n            return f\"{class_name_local}.{strongest_ext_obj.get_name()}\"\n\n        def _find_strongest_extension_object(self, extension_names_local):\n            strongest_extension = None\n            max_strength = -float('inf')\n\n            for ext_name in extension_names_local:\n                current_strength = self._calculator.calculate_strength(ext_name)\n                current_extension = Extension(ext_name, current_strength)\n\n                if current_strength > max_strength:\n                    max_strength = current_strength\n                    strongest_extension = current_extension\n\n            return strongest_extension\n\n    calculator = ExtensionCalculator()\n    finder = StrongestExtensionFinder(calculator)\n    return finder.find(class_name, extensions)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/156", "solution": "```python\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer, obtain its roman numeral equivalent as a string,\n    and return it in lowercase.\n    Restrictions: 1 <= num <= 1000\n    \"\"\"\n    ROMAN_MAP = [\n        (1000, \"M\"), (900, \"CM\"), (500, \"D\"), (400, \"CD\"),\n        (100, \"C\"), (90, \"XC\"), (50, \"L\"), (40, \"XL\"),\n        (10, \"X\"), (9, \"IX\"), (5, \"V\"), (4, \"IV\"), (1, \"I\")\n    ]\n\n    result = []\n    temp_num = number\n    for value, symbol in ROMAN_MAP:\n        while temp_num >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            temp_num -= value\n    return \"\".join(result).lower()\n```"}
