{"task_id": "HumanEval/160", "solution": "class AlgebraEvaluator:\n    VALID_OPERATORS = {'+', '-', '*', '//', '**'}\n\n    def evaluate_expression(self, operators: list[str], operands: list[int]) -> int:\n        self._validate_inputs(operators, operands)\n        expression_string = self._build_expression_string(operators, operands)\n        return int(eval(expression_string))\n\n    def _validate_inputs(self, operators: list[str], operands: list[int]) -> None:\n        if not operators or not operands:\n            raise ValueError(\"Operator and operand lists cannot be empty.\")\n        if len(operators) != len(operands) - 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Length of operator list must be one less than operand list.\")\n        if len(operands) < 2:\n            raise ValueError(\"Operand list must contain at least two operands.\")\n\n        for op in operators:\n            if not isinstance(op, str) or op not in self.VALID_OPERATORS:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Invalid operator found: {op}\")\n        for num in operands:\n            if not isinstance(num, int) or num < 0:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Invalid operand found: {num}. Operands must be non-negative integers.\")\n\n    def _build_expression_string(self, operators: list[str], operands: list[int]) -> str:\n        expression_parts = []\n        for i in range(len(operators)):\n            expression_parts.append(str(operands[i]))\n            expression_parts.append(operators[i])\n        expression_parts.append(str(operands[-1]))\n        return \" \".join(expression_parts)\n\n\ndef do_algebra(operator, operand):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two lists operator, and operand. The first list has basic algebra operations, and\n    the second list is a list of integers. Use the two given lists to build the algebric\n    expression and return the evaluation of this expression.\n\n    The basic algebra operations:\n    Addition ( + )\n    Subtraction ( - )\n    Multiplication ( * )\n    Floor division ( // )\n    Exponentiation ( ** )\n\n    Example:\n    operator['+', '*', '-']\n    array = [2, 3, 4, 5]\n    result = 2 + 3 * 4 - 5\n    => result = 9\n\n    Note:\n        The length of operator list is equal to the length of operand list minus one.\n        Operand is a list of of non-negative integers.\n        Operator list has at least one operator, and operand list has at least two operands.\n\n    \"\"\"\n    evaluator = AlgebraEvaluator()\n    return evaluator.evaluate_expression(operator, operand)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/161", "solution": "```python\ndef solve(s):\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    if s[i] is a letter, reverse its case from lower to upper or vise versa,\n    otherwise keep it as it is.\n    If the string contains no letters, reverse the string.\n    The function should return the resulted string.\n    Examples\n    solve(\"1234\") = \"4321\"\n    solve(\"ab\") = \"AB\"\n    solve(\"#a@C\") = \"#A@c\"\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_letter(char):\n        return char.isalpha()\n\n    def _reverse_case(char):\n        if char.islower():\n            return char.upper()\n        elif char.isupper():\n            return char.lower()\n        return char # Fallback, though typically called only for letters\n\n    def _contains_letters(string_to_check):\n        for char in string_to_check:\n            if _is_letter(char):\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    if not _contains_letters(s):\n        return s[::-1]\n    else:\n        result_chars = []\n        for char in s:\n            if _is_letter(char):\n                result_chars.append(_reverse_case(char))\n            else:\n                result_chars.append(char)\n        return \"\".join(result_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/163", "solution": "```python\ndef generate_integers(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two positive integers a and b, return the even integers between a\n    and b, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    min_val = min(a, b)\n    max_val = max(a, b)\n\n    even_numbers = []\n    for num in range(min_val, max_val + 1):\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            even_numbers.append(num)\n\n    return even_numbers\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "def right_angle_triangle(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or\n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    '''\n    # A small constant for floating-point comparisons.\n    EPSILON = 1e-9\n\n    # Square the side lengths, ensuring they are float for consistent calculations.\n    a_sq = float(a)**2\n    b_sq = float(b)**2\n    c_sq = float(c)**2\n\n    # Sort the squared sides to easily apply the Pythagorean theorem.\n    # The sum of the two smaller squares should equal the largest square.\n    sides_squared = sorted([a_sq, b_sq, c_sq])\n\n    # Check if a^2 + b^2 = c^2 within the defined epsilon for floating point accuracy.\n    return abs(sides_squared[0] + sides_squared[1] - sides_squared[2]) < EPSILON"}
